## Overview of Recent Recalls

As of June 17th, 2026, the food industry has experienced 577 recalls this year alone--a staggering number when compared to historical averages. Within this, 176 were classified as Class I (life-threatening), while 361 fell into Class II (moderate risk), and 39 as Class III (minor risk). The prevalence of recalls is a stark reminder of the industry's ongoing struggle with quality control and regulatory compliance.

## Focus on Undeclared Color Additives

This week, Galaxy RG Corp finds itself in the spotlight as it becomes the subject of multiple recalls related to undeclared color additives, including Yellow #5, Yellow #6, and Red #40. A total of eight products, ranging from cakes to cheesecakes, have been pulled from shelves due to improper labeling. These additives, while common, require clear declaration due to possible allergic reactions or other sensitivities among consumers.

Refresqueria Estrella Inc. has also joined the ranks with a Class III recall for its 'Passion Fruit Drink,' which contained undeclared Red #40.

## Repeat Offenders

A closer inspection of annual trends reveals 87 repeat offenders among manufacturers this year alone. Of particular note is Galaxy RG Corp, which has seen multiple recalls in recent months, casting doubt on its ability to maintain acceptable compliance standards.

## The Bigger Picture

Undeclared allergens, improper labeling, and contamination continue to plague the food industry, with 283 allergen-related recalls leading the pack this year, followed closely by Salmonella with 173 instances. Mislabeling remains the least common reason for recalls, with only four cases reported.

## Consumer Advice

Stay vigilant and informed. As colorful as cakes and drinks may seem, it's crucial to remember that transparency in labeling is not just a regulatory requirement but a moral obligation to consumer safety.
